      "latency_ms": 2208.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Three States)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we typically drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Chemical Superpowers)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid on Earth:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (in our blood, through plant roots, etc.).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass, and crucially, why lakes freeze from the top down rather than the bottom up, allowing fish and aquatic life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the temperature of the entire planet, and why coastal areas have milder climates than inland areas.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of a tall tree against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n"
